# History, activity and statistics

Say what each figure measures, over which period and from which source. History shows what entities reported over a chosen period. Activity records events. Statistics summarise numeric readings. None of them is covered by the civica Energy chart adapter, even when the theme colours match.

The examples on this page are simulated. They do not connect to Home Assistant or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-011-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Surface | What to keep |
| --- | --- |
| History panel | The chosen targets, filters, period, native zoom and any export. The Recorder decides what history is stored. |
| History graph | State changes and numeric readings in their own units. Do not turn a categorical state into a smoothed numeric line. |
| Activity | An event is not a continuous measurement and does not prove that a physical action finished. Keep the event time and the real attribution. |
| Statistics graph | The chosen statistic and interval. Mean, minimum, maximum, sum, state and change are different things and are not interchangeable. |
| Download or data equivalent | The real scope, dates, units and what a gap means. Do not offer an export that does not exist. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-011-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Show these cases separately: no targets chosen, a successful period with no records, an entity excluded from the Recorder, unavailable source data and a failed request. Never turn any of them into a flat zero line. A recent entity may have no earlier records. Home Assistant keeps 5-minute statistics for the period set in the Recorder configuration and hourly statistics indefinitely, so a shorter raw history can still leave longer-term statistics.

Keep a gap apart from a real zero. A reading of 0 W is a measurement. A missing interval is no measurement, so leave it blank or label it “No data”, and do not draw it as zero.

Keep power apart from energy. Power, in W or kW, is a rate at a moment. Energy, in kWh, is an amount accumulated over time. Home Assistant statistics keep the minimum, maximum and mean for sensors with a `measurement` state class, and a running sum for metered sensors with a `total` or `total_increasing` state class. So:

- A mean in kW is not an amount in kWh. Do not label it energy.
- A kWh total for a period is not the power now. Do not label it a current reading.
- Name the quantity and unit on every axis, row and heading, and say whether a figure is a reading, a mean or a sum.

When the person changes the date, keep the previous period label on any retained plot until the new response is ready. Never show a new period heading over old values. If a request fails, keep the chosen targets and period visible and label retained values with their original period.

Keep gaps, signs and units intact. A graph of mean temperature is not a list of every original reading. When comparing two periods, name both periods and say how much of each has data before you calculate a change. Never infer from a missing attribution that someone pressed a physical button.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-011-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Give each figure its quantity, period and source. Call a time an observation, an event or the edge of an interval, whichever it is. Use the person’s date, number and time zone preferences for dynamic content. A fixed reference table states its own date and time zone so it still makes sense after publication.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-011-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Test the native keyboard and non-visual routes on the installed frontend. A hover tooltip alone does not give exact values. Use the native export, or an equivalent you have actually built, and do not promise the Energy data table here. Where a chart has no usable route to its values, log it with steps to reproduce rather than calling a themed chart accessible. Keep zoom and source controls named and usable at narrow widths.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-011-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

civica does not target the History panel or its chart hosts. Its chart adapter covers only the Energy dashboard cards described in [Energy and history](../patterns/energy.html). Keep the native History, Activity and statistics behaviour and the supported theme variables. The tables on this page are design references, not a data-fetching adapter. Before any future adapter is accepted, it must document its source and statistic, who owns period changes and teardown, and the accessible alternative it adds.

